OAI_PAPER({"slug": "Conditional-coordinate-sweeps-and-analytic-transfer-September-26-2026", "title": "Conditional coordinate sweeps and analytic transfer", "pdf": "https://github.com/openai/math/blob/main/preprints/Conditional-coordinate-sweeps-and-analytic-transfer-September-26-2026/main.pdf", "family": "238", "family_title": "Optimal logarithmic mixing of the Thorp shuffle", "abstract_html": "For coordinate sweeps with near-uniform line laws and line sizes among powers of two in a suitable fixed interval, we prove a Schatten-moment bound after conditioning on any feasible collection of prescribed card trajectories. An analytic transfer to binary sweeps then shows that a fixed number of sweeps mixes the Thorp shuffle on \\(2^d\\) cards in \\(O(d)\\) physical steps, matching the order of the support lower bound.", "blocks": [{"label": "Theorem 1", "orig": "Theorem 1.1 (Binary sweep contraction)", "depth": 0, "snippet": "There are absolute constants \\(g>0\\) and \\(d_0\\) such that, for \\(d\\ge d_0\\) and every \\(\\lambda\\vdash 2^d\\), \\( \\|K_\\lambda\\|_{\\mathrm{op}}\\le D_\\lambda^{-g}. \\) The sign representation is annihilated.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(d\\) be a positive integer and \\(N=2^d\\).</p><p class=\"li\">A binary coordinate sweep on \\(\\{0,1\\}^d\\) visits the coordinates in order. In each coordinate direction, independently on every parallel edge, it fixes or swaps the two cards with probability \\(1/2\\) each. Denote its permutation law by \\(B_N\\).</p><p class=\"li\">For the irreducible unitary representation \\(\\rho_\\lambda\\) of \\(S_N\\), indexed by \\(\\lambda\\vdash N\\), put\n  \\[\n  K_\\lambda=\\mathbb E_{g\\sim B_N}\\rho_\\lambda(g),\n  \\qquad D_\\lambda=\\dim\\rho_\\lambda.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">For probability measures on \\(S_N\\), use\n  \\[\n  \\|\\mu-\\nu\\|_{\\mathrm{TV}}\n  =\\frac12\\sum_{g\\in S_N}|\\mu(g)-\\nu(g)|.\n  \\]\n  Write \\(U_N\\) for the uniform probability on \\(S_N\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There are absolute constants \\(g&gt;0\\) and \\(d_0\\) such that, for \\(d\\ge d_0\\) and every \\(\\lambda\\vdash 2^d\\),\n\\[\n\\|K_\\lambda\\|_{\\mathrm{op}}\\le D_\\lambda^{-g}.\n\\]\nThe sign representation is annihilated. There is an absolute positive integer \\(w\\) such that the law after \\(w\\) independent sweeps converges to uniform in total variation as \\(d\\to\\infty\\), uniformly over deterministic initial decks.</p>", "proof": "<p>Let \\(\\ell=\\log_2R\\), where \\(R\\) is fixed by Proposition 6. For \\(d\\ge\\ell\\), write\n\\[\nd=b\\ell+t,\n\\qquad 0\\le t&lt;\\ell.\n\\]\nSplit the consecutive bits into one block of \\(\\ell+t\\) bits and \\(b-1\\) blocks of \\(\\ell\\) bits. Their line sizes lie in \\([R,R^2]\\).</p><p>At \\(z=1\\), the binary sweeps on disjoint lines within a block can be interleaved by bit direction. Thus the grid sweep is exactly \\(B_N\\), for \\(N=2^d\\). Proposition 7 gives\n\\[\n\\|K_\\lambda\\|_{\\mathrm{op}}\\le D_\\lambda^{-g}\n\\]\nfor all \\(\\lambda\\vdash N\\), with absolute \\(g&gt;0\\). This proves the operator assertion after choosing \\(d_0\\).</p><p>Its sign coefficient is zero: the sign of any one fair switch is independent of all remaining switches and has mean zero. Proposition 8 therefore gives an absolute number \\(w\\) of sweeps whose law converges to uniform in total variation, uniformly over deterministic initial decks.</p><p>For the physical interpretation, one Thorp shuffle acts on binary position strings as\n\\[\n(x_1,x_2,\\ldots,x_d)\n\\longmapsto\n(x_2,\\ldots,x_d,x_1+\\xi_{x_2,\\ldots,x_d}),\n\\]\nwith addition modulo two and independent fair bits \\(\\xi\\). Undoing the deterministic coordinate rotation makes successive switch layers act in successive coordinate directions. After \\(d\\) physical shuffles the rotation is the identity, so one binary sweep costs exactly \\(d\\) physical steps. Consequently, for all sufficiently large \\(d\\), the distance is at most \\(1/4\\) after \\(wd\\) physical steps, and\n\\[\nt_{\\mathrm{mix}}(d)=O(d).\n\\]</p><p>In each fixed dimension, convergence also holds. A sweep can be the identity or any single cube-edge transposition with positive probability, and the edge transpositions of a connected graph generate \\(S_N\\). Padding products with identities gives a power of the sweep law with full support. Its positive minimum probability gives a uniform minorization and convergence by iteration.</p>"}, {"label": "Theorem 2", "orig": "Proposition 1.2 (Support obstruction)", "depth": 0, "snippet": "For every integer \\(t\\ge0\\), \\( \\|q_d^{*t}-U_N\\|_{\\mathrm{TV}} \\ge 1-\\frac{2^{tN/2}}{N!}. \\) Consequently, \\( t_{\\mathrm{mix}}(d) \\ge \\left\\lceil\\frac2N\\log_2\\left(\\frac{3N!}{4}\\right)\\right\\rceil =2d-O(1). \\)", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(N=2^d\\).</p><p class=\"li\">A physical Thorp shuffle splits the deck into equal halves, pairs corresponding cards, and uses an independent fair coin to order each pair before interleaving.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(q_d\\) be its one-step permutation law and \\(U_N\\) the uniform probability on \\(S_N\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Define\n  \\[\n  t_{\\mathrm{mix}}(d)\n  =\\inf\\left\\{t\\in\\mathbb Z_{\\ge0}:\n  \\|q_d^{*t}-U_N\\|_{\\mathrm{TV}}\\le\\frac14\\right\\}.\n  \\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For every integer \\(t\\ge0\\),\n\\[\n\\|q_d^{*t}-U_N\\|_{\\mathrm{TV}}\n\\ge 1-\\frac{2^{tN/2}}{N!}.\n\\]\nConsequently,\n\\[\nt_{\\mathrm{mix}}(d)\n\\ge\n\\left\\lceil\\frac2N\\log_2\\left(\\frac{3N!}{4}\\right)\\right\\rceil\n=2d-O(1).\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>The \\(tN/2\\) fair bits determine the permutation, so its support has at most \\(2^{tN/2}\\) elements. Testing that support in total variation gives\n\\[\n\\|q_d^{*t}-U_N\\|_{\\mathrm{TV}}\n\\ge1-\\frac{2^{tN/2}}{N!}.\n\\]\nDistance at most \\(1/4\\) requires the uniform mass of this support to be at least \\(3/4\\). Therefore\n\\[\n2^{tN/2}\\ge\\frac{3N!}{4},\n\\]\nwhich proves the integer bound\n\\[\nt_{\\mathrm{mix}}(d)\n\\ge\n\\left\\lceil\\frac2N\\log_2\\left(\\frac{3N!}{4}\\right)\\right\\rceil.\n\\]\nStirling’s formula gives \\(2d-O(1)\\).</p><p>Together with the \\(O(d)\\) physical-step upper bound established in Theorem 1, this yields\n\\[\nt_{\\mathrm{mix}}(d)=\\Theta(d).\n\\]</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 1", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "For sufficiently large \\(l\\), put \\( k=\\min(l-\\lambda_1,l-\\lambda'_1). \\) If \\(0<k\\le l/3\\), then \\( D_\\lambda\\ge e^{-1}\\binom lk. \\) If \\(k>l/3\\), then \\( F(\\lambda)\\ge c_{\\mathrm{dim}}l \\) for an absolute constant …", "statement": "<p>Representation conventions and dimension estimates from Section 2.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">A permutation sends each input position to its output, and a product \\(gh\\) applies \\(h\\) first. Thus\n  \\[\n  \\widehat{\\mu*\\nu}(\\lambda)=\\widehat\\mu(\\lambda)\\widehat\\nu(\\lambda),\n  \\qquad\n  \\widehat\\mu(\\lambda)=\\sum_g\\mu(g)\\rho_\\lambda(g).\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">All representations are over \\(\\mathbb C\\). For \\(\\lambda\\vdash l\\), write \\([\\lambda]\\) for the irreducible representation of \\(S_l\\),\n  \\[\n  D_\\lambda=\\dim[\\lambda],\n  \\qquad F(\\lambda)=\\log D_\\lambda.\n  \\]\n  The empty diagram has dimension one. All logarithms are natural unless a base is specified.</p><p class=\"li\">Use unnormalized Schatten norms:\n  \\[\n  \\|A\\|_r^r=\\operatorname{Tr}|A|^r,\n  \\qquad |A|=(A^*A)^{1/2}.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Changing the input or output identification of a random bijection with a reference slot set multiplies its representation average by unitaries on the two sides; its singular values therefore do not change.</p><p class=\"li\">The classical representation facts used below are:</p><p class=\"li l2 num\">1. \\(D_\\lambda\\) counts standard tableaux and is given by the hook-length formula.</p><p class=\"li l2 num\">2. Conjugating \\(\\lambda\\) twists \\([\\lambda]\\) by sign.</p><p class=\"li l2 num\">3. Restriction from \\(S_l\\) to \\(S_{l-j}\\) gives \\([\\sigma]\\) multiplicity equal to the number of standard tableaux of \\(\\lambda/\\sigma\\).</p><p class=\"li l2 num\">4. The representation on placements of \\(j\\) distinguishable cards in \\(l\\) slots contains \\([\\lambda]\\) exactly when \\(\\lambda_1\\ge l-j\\).</p><p class=\"li l2 num\">5. Pieri’s rule adds a horizontal strip when inducing with a trivial representation and a vertical strip when inducing with a sign representation.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For sufficiently large \\(l\\), put\n\\[\nk=\\min(l-\\lambda_1,l-\\lambda'_1).\n\\]\nIf \\(0&lt;k\\le l/3\\), then\n\\[\nD_\\lambda\\ge e^{-1}\\binom lk. \\tag{1}\n\\]\nIf \\(k&gt;l/3\\), then\n\\[\nF(\\lambda)\\ge c_{\\mathrm{dim}}l\n\\]\nfor an absolute constant \\(c_{\\mathrm{dim}}&gt;0\\). Reducing that constant if necessary,\n\\[\nc_{\\mathrm{dim}}k\\le F(\\lambda)\\le k\\log l\n\\]\nfor every \\(k\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>If \\(0&lt;k\\le l/3\\), transpose if necessary so that the first row has length \\(l-k\\). Let \\(\\mu\\) be the diagram of \\(k\\) boxes below it. The hook formula gives\n\\[\nD_\\lambda\n=\\binom lk D_\\mu\n\\prod_{j=1}^{l-k}\n\\left(1+\\frac{\\mu'_j}{l-k-j+1}\\right)^{-1}.\n\\]\nOnly \\(j\\le k\\) can contribute a nontrivial factor. Since \\(\\sum_j\\mu'_j=k\\), the correction product is at least\n\\[\n\\exp\\left(-\\frac{k}{l-2k}\\right)\\ge e^{-1},\n\\]\nproving (1).</p><p>Suppose \\(k&gt;l/3\\). If both the first row and first column have length below \\(l/8\\), every hook is shorter than \\(l/4\\), and the hook formula with Stirling’s bound gives \\(F(\\lambda)\\ge c_{\\mathrm{dim}}l\\). Otherwise transpose if needed so that the first row has length \\(v\\in[l/8,2l/3]\\). Retain that row and a subdiagram of \\(\\lfloor v/4\\rfloor\\) boxes below it. The preceding large-row bound makes the dimension of this retained shape exponential in \\(v\\), hence in \\(l\\). Branching shows that \\(D_\\lambda\\) is at least that dimension.</p><p>Reducing \\(c_{\\mathrm{dim}}\\) gives \\(F(\\lambda)\\ge c_{\\mathrm{dim}}k\\) in every case. Conversely, the placement criterion, with a sign twist if necessary, places \\([\\lambda]\\) inside a representation of dimension\n\\[\n(l)_k\\le l^k.\n\\]\nThus \\(F(\\lambda)\\le k\\log l\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 2", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Both \\(U_m\\) and \\(B_m\\) send a single slot uniformly to the line and have expected sign zero.", "statement": "<p>Conditional trajectories and their potential, from Section 3.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Fix a power of two \\(R\\ge2\\). Let\n  \\[\n  \\Omega=\\Omega_1\\times\\cdots\\times\\Omega_b,\n  \\qquad m_j=|\\Omega_j|\\in[R,R^2],\n  \\qquad s=|\\Omega|=\\prod_jm_j,\n  \\]\n  where \\(b\\ge1\\) and every \\(m_j\\) is a power of two.</p><p class=\"li\">Each \\(\\Omega_j\\) is identified with \\(\\{0,1\\}^{\\log_2m_j}\\).</p><p class=\"li\">A sweep independently permutes every coordinate-\\(1\\) line, then every coordinate-\\(2\\) line, and so on. A line belongs to its particular stage.</p><p class=\"li\">On a line of size \\(m\\), use\n  \\[\n  L_m(z)=(1-z)U_m+zB_m, \\tag{2}\n  \\]\n  where \\(U_m\\) is uniform on \\(S_m\\) and \\(B_m\\) is the binary sweep law.</p><p class=\"li\">Let\n  \\[\n  A_R=\\max_m\\max_{g\\in S_m}m!B_m(g),\n  \\qquad\n  z_{\\mathrm{cmp}}(R)=\\min\\left\\{\\frac12,\\frac1{A_R+1}\\right\\},\n  \\]\n  with \\(m\\) ranging over the allowed line sizes.</p><p class=\"li\">Throughout this proposition, \\(0\\le z\\le z_{\\mathrm{cmp}}(R)\\).</p><p class=\"li\">A path from \\(u=(u_1,\\ldots,u_b)\\) to \\(v=(v_1,\\ldots,v_b)\\) occupies after stage \\(j\\) the slot\n  \\[\n  (v_1,\\ldots,v_j,u_{j+1},\\ldots,u_b),\n  \\qquad 0\\le j\\le b.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(\\mathcal H\\) prescribe \\(h\\) such paths with distinct slots at every layer, including input and output. These occupied slots are called holes.</p><p class=\"li\">For a stage line \\(L\\), let \\(h_L\\) count prescribed paths using it, and let \\(E_L(\\mathcal H)\\) be the event that its line permutation realizes their prescribed assignments.</p><p class=\"li\">Put \\(M=s-h\\). After removing the prescribed paths, write \\(K_\\lambda^{\\mathcal H}\\), for \\(\\lambda\\vdash M\\), for the representation average of the remaining random bijection under fixed input and output identifications.</p><p class=\"li\">Define\n  \\[\n  \\phi_m(u)=\\log\\frac{m^u}{(m)_u},\n  \\qquad\n  (m)_u=m(m-1)\\cdots(m-u+1),\n  \\qquad (m)_0=1,\n  \\]\n  and\n  \\[\n  C(\\mathcal H)=\\sum_{\\text{stage lines }L}\\phi_{|L|}(h_L).\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">For placements \\(x,y\\) of \\(t\\) distinguishable cards in the free input and output slots, let \\(p_{\\mathcal H}(x,y)\\) be their conditional transition probability. If it is positive, their endpoint pairs determine an augmented disjoint path family \\(\\mathcal H^+\\) of size \\(h+t\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>Both \\(U_m\\) and \\(B_m\\) send a single slot uniformly to the line and have expected sign zero. Moreover,\n\\[\n\\frac12U_m\\le L_m(z)\\le2U_m \\tag{3}\n\\]\nas measures. Thus every prescribed layer-disjoint family is feasible, and conditioning leaves independent residual line laws, each conditioned on its prescribed assignments.</p><p>The potential satisfies\n\\[\n0\\le\\phi_m(u)\\le u,\n\\qquad\n0\\le C(\\mathcal H)\\le bh.\n\\]\nFor every positive transition probability,\n\\[\np_{\\mathcal H}(x,y)\n\\le\ns^{-t}\n\\exp\\left\\{\nC(\\mathcal H^+)-C(\\mathcal H)+(\\log4)tb\n\\right\\}. \\tag{4}\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>The one-card marginal of a binary sweep is uniform. Toggling one independent fair switch reverses its sign, so \\(B_m\\) has expected sign zero; the assertions for \\(U_m\\) are immediate. There are finitely many allowed line sizes, so \\(z_{\\mathrm{cmp}}(R)&gt;0\\), and its definition gives (3).</p><p>Layer disjointness makes the prescribed assignments on each line injective, and (3) makes \\(E_L(\\mathcal H)\\) have positive probability. The conditioning event is the intersection of these events over independent lines. The conditioned residual laws therefore remain independent.</p><p>The summands in\n\\[\n\\phi_m(u)=\\sum_{r=0}^{u-1}\\log\\frac m{m-r}\n\\]\nare nonnegative and increasing. For \\(u\\ge1\\), their mean over the first \\(u\\) terms is at most their mean over all \\(m\\) terms, which is at most one because \\(m!\\ge(m/e)^m\\). The case \\(u=0\\) is immediate. Since the \\(h_L\\) sum to \\(h\\) at every stage, \\(C(\\mathcal H)\\le bh\\).</p><p>For a line used by the added paths, put \\(m=|L|\\) and let \\(v_L&gt;0\\) count those paths. Its conditional probability is bounded by\n\\[\n\\frac{L_m(z)(E_L(\\mathcal H^+))}\n     {L_m(z)(E_L(\\mathcal H))}\n\\le \\frac4{(m-h_L)_{v_L}}.\n\\]\nThe corresponding uniform ratio is exactly \\((m-h_L)_{v_L}^{-1}\\); (3) changes numerator and denominator by factors between \\(1/2\\) and \\(2\\). Also,\n\\[\n\\frac1{(m-h_L)_{v_L}}\n=\nm^{-v_L}\n\\exp\\{\\phi_m(h_L+v_L)-\\phi_m(h_L)\\}.\n\\]\nUnused lines contribute one, and at most \\(tb\\) lines are used. Each added path contributes \\(\\prod_jm_j^{-1}=s^{-1}\\). Multiplication gives (4).</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 3", "orig": "Lemma 4.1 (Conditional sparse contraction)", "depth": 0, "snippet": "There are \\(\\rho>0\\) and \\(R_0\\) such that, for each power of two \\(R\\ge R_0\\), one can choose \\(z_{\\mathrm{sp}}(R)>0\\) with the following property.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">For a power of two \\(R\\), consider every ordered grid\n  \\[\n  \\Omega=\\prod_{j=1}^b\\Omega_j,\n  \\qquad |\\Omega_j|=m_j\\in[R,R^2],\n  \\qquad s=\\prod_jm_j,\n  \\]\n  with all \\(m_j\\) powers of two.</p><p class=\"li\">The independent stage-line laws are \\(L_m(z)=(1-z)U_m+zB_m\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(\\mathcal H\\) prescribe \\(h\\) paths with distinct slots at every layer, and let \\(K_\\lambda^{\\mathcal H}\\) be the conditional average of the remaining bijection in \\([\\lambda]\\), where \\(M=s-h\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Fix \\(0&lt;\\theta&lt;1\\) and \\(K_0\\ge1\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There are \\(\\rho&gt;0\\) and \\(R_0\\) such that, for each power of two \\(R\\ge R_0\\), one can choose \\(z_{\\mathrm{sp}}(R)&gt;0\\) with the following property. If \\(k\\ge1\\) is an integer satisfying\n\\[\nh\\le K_0k,\n\\qquad h+k\\le s^{1-\\theta}, \\tag{6}\n\\]\nand \\(\\lambda\\vdash M\\) satisfies\n\\[\n\\lambda_1=M-k\n\\quad\\text{or}\\quad\n\\lambda'_1=M-k,\n\\]\nthen, for every \\(z\\in[0,z_{\\mathrm{sp}}(R)]\\),\n\\[\n\\|K_\\lambda^{\\mathcal H}\\|_{\\mathrm{op}}\n\\le s^{-\\rho k}. \\tag{7}\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Put \\(B_0=\\lceil8/\\theta\\rceil\\). Lemmas 3.1–3.3 give, for \\(b&gt;B_0\\), the row bound \\(s^{-\\theta k/16}\\), using only the line comparison (3). Lemma 3.6 gives zero for the column operator throughout that parameter interval.</p><p>For \\(b\\le B_0\\), Lemmas 3.4 and 3.5 at \\(z=0\\) give, with\n\\[\n\\beta=\\min\\{\\xi/4,\\nu/2\\}&gt;0,\n\\]\nthe row bound\n\\[\n2e^{Cb(1+K_0)k}s^{-\\beta k}\n\\le s^{-\\beta k/2}\n\\]\nfor all sufficiently large \\(R\\), because \\(b/\\log s\\le1/\\log R\\). The column operator is zero at \\(z=0\\).</p><p>Choose\n\\[\n\\rho=\\min\\{\\theta/32,\\beta/4\\}&gt;0\n\\]\nbefore \\(R\\), and then choose \\(R_0\\) large enough for all estimates. For each \\(R\\ge R_0\\), the bounded-coordinate bounds at zero have strict slack relative to \\(s^{-\\rho k}\\).</p><p>Fix such an \\(R\\). For \\(b\\le B_0\\), there are finitely many allowed grids, with \\(s\\le R^{2B_0}\\), and finitely many feasible path families, integers \\(k\\), and partitions. Every conditional operator is a finite expression in line probabilities with conditioning denominators positive at zero, hence is continuous there. Strict slack and finiteness give \\(\\delta(R)&gt;0\\) such that (7) holds for these operators when \\(0\\le z\\le\\delta(R)\\).</p><p>Set\n\\[\nz_{\\mathrm{sp}}(R)\n=\\min\\{\\delta(R),z_{\\mathrm{cmp}}(R)\\}&gt;0.\n\\]\nThe many-coordinate proof applies throughout this interval by (3), completing the proposition.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 3.1", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "\\( \\|K_\\lambda^{\\mathcal H}\\|_{\\mathrm{op}}\\le\\|Q\\|_{\\mathrm{HS}}, \\) and \\( |s^kQ(x,y)| \\le 2^k\\exp\\{bh+(1+\\log4)bk\\}. \\) Extend \\(Q\\) by zero when either endpoint array fails to be an injection into its available …", "statement": "<p>Alternating placement kernel.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use the grid, path family, and sparse assumptions of Proposition 3.</p><p class=\"li\">Assume \\(0\\le z\\le z_{\\mathrm{cmp}}(R)\\) and \\(\\lambda_1=M-k\\).</p><p class=\"li\">For input and output \\(k\\)-placements \\(x,y\\), and \\(A\\subseteq[k]\\), let \\(p_A(x_A,y_A)\\) be the conditional probability of the paths for labels in \\(A\\), with \\(p_\\varnothing=1\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Define\n  \\[\n  Q(x,y)=s^{-k}\\sum_{A\\subseteq[k]}\n  (-1)^{k-|A|}s^{|A|}p_A(x_A,y_A). \\tag{8}\n  \\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>\\[\n\\|K_\\lambda^{\\mathcal H}\\|_{\\mathrm{op}}\\le\\|Q\\|_{\\mathrm{HS}},\n\\]\nand\n\\[\n|s^kQ(x,y)|\n\\le\n2^k\\exp\\{bh+(1+\\log4)bk\\}. \\tag{9}\n\\]</p><p>Extend \\(Q\\) by zero when either endpoint array fails to be an injection into its available slots. If all \\(2k\\) endpoints are then sampled independently and uniformly from \\(\\Omega\\), with replacement,\n\\[\n\\|Q\\|_{\\mathrm{HS}}^2\n=\ns^{-2k}\\sum_{x,y\\in\\Omega^k}|s^kQ(x,y)|^2\n=\n\\mathbb E_{\\mathrm{full}}|s^kQ(x,y)|^2.\n\\]\nEvery sampled pair of endpoints determines an auxiliary path; at any fixed layer its slot is uniform in \\(\\Omega\\), independently between particles.</p>", "proof": "<p>The \\(A=[k]\\) term is the full placement transition kernel. For a proper subset \\(A\\), the corresponding operator has output range among pullbacks \\(y\\mapsto f(y_A)\\) of functions on \\(|A|\\)-placements. By branching, this subrepresentation contains only types with first row at least\n\\[\nM-|A|&gt;M-k.\n\\]\nProjection to type \\(\\lambda\\) kills every proper-subset term. The full placement representation contains \\([\\lambda]\\), so its surviving block is a copy of \\(K_\\lambda^{\\mathcal H}\\). Consequently,\n\\[\n\\|K_\\lambda^{\\mathcal H}\\|_{\\mathrm{op}}\\le\\|Q\\|_{\\mathrm{HS}}.\n\\]</p><p>If the paths of \\(A\\) augment \\(\\mathcal H\\) to a disjoint family \\(\\mathcal H_A\\), Proposition 2 gives\n\\[\ns^{|A|}p_A\n\\le\n\\exp\\{C(\\mathcal H_A)-C(\\mathcal H)+(\\log4)|A|b\\}\n\\le\n\\exp\\{bh+(1+\\log4)bk\\}.\n\\]\nInvalid subsets have probability zero. Summing over \\(A\\) proves (9).</p><p>On endpoint placements retain (8), even when the full family collides at an intermediate layer. The zero extension gives the displayed Hilbert–Schmidt identity. Uniformity and independence between particles follow from the coordinate formula for their auxiliary paths.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 3.2", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "For \\(0\\le w\\le k\\), the probability that at least \\(w\\) particles are incident to sharing is at most \\( \\sum_{j=\\lceil w/2\\rceil}^k\\binom kjr^j \\le2^kr^{w/2}. \\)", "statement": "<p>Rooted-forest sharing count.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Sample the endpoints of \\(k\\) auxiliary particle paths independently and uniformly from the full grid, with replacement.</p><p class=\"li\">Keep \\(h\\) prescribed hole paths fixed.</p><p class=\"li\">Sharing means either a common slot at a layer or a common line at a stage.</p><p class=\"li\">Suppose \\(\\omega\\) bounds the probability that one independent particle shares with any fixed trajectory.</p><p class=\"li\">Put \\(r=(k+h)\\omega\\), and assume \\(r\\le1\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For \\(0\\le w\\le k\\), the probability that at least \\(w\\) particles are incident to sharing is at most\n\\[\n\\sum_{j=\\lceil w/2\\rceil}^k\\binom kjr^j\n\\le2^kr^{w/2}. \\tag{10}\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Form the graph whose vertices are the particles and holes, with an edge for every sharing involving a particle. If at least \\(w\\) particles are incident to edges, the graph contains a directed forest with at least \\(\\lceil w/2\\rceil\\) edges and distinct particle tails.</p><p>In a component meeting holes, point each particle to a neighbor one step closer to the nearest hole. Distances decrease, so these edges form a forest rooted at holes, with every particle a tail. In a component containing only particles, with \\(v\\ge2\\) incident vertices, a rooted spanning tree supplies \\(v-1\\ge v/2\\) particle tails.</p><p>For a fixed directed forest with \\(j\\) edges, condition on all paths except a leaf particle. Its parent is fixed, its sharing event has probability at most \\(\\omega\\), and it occurs in no other remaining edge. Removing leaves successively bounds the probability by \\(\\omega^j\\). There are at most\n\\[\n\\binom kj(k+h)^j\n\\]\nchoices of tails and parents. Summing and using \\(r\\le1\\) proves (10).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 3.3", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "\\( \\Pr_{\\mathrm{full}}(Q\\ne0)\\le2^ks^{-\\theta k/4}, \\qquad \\|Q\\|_{\\mathrm{HS}}\\le s^{-\\theta k/16}. \\)", "statement": "<p>Many-coordinate row contraction.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use the assumptions of Proposition 3 and the row case of Lemma 3.1.</p><p class=\"li\">Put \\(B_0=\\lceil8/\\theta\\rceil\\), assume \\(b&gt;B_0\\), and take \\(R\\) sufficiently large in terms of \\(\\theta,K_0\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(0\\le z\\le z_{\\mathrm{cmp}}(R)\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>\\[\n\\Pr_{\\mathrm{full}}(Q\\ne0)\\le2^ks^{-\\theta k/4},\n\\qquad\n\\|Q\\|_{\\mathrm{HS}}\\le s^{-\\theta k/16}.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Take sharing to mean a common stage line. A particle uses a fixed line at stage \\(j\\) with probability \\(m_j/s\\), so\n\\[\n\\omega\\le\\frac{b\\max_jm_j}{s}.\n\\]\nSince \\(\\max_jm_j\\le s^{2/b}\\),\n\\[\n(k+h)\\omega\n\\le bs^{-\\theta+2/b}\n\\le s^{-\\theta/2}\n\\]\nfor sufficiently large \\(R\\), uniformly in \\(b&gt;B_0\\). Here \\(2/b&lt;\\theta/4\\), and \\(b\\le s^{\\theta/4}\\) follows uniformly from \\(s\\ge R^b\\) after increasing \\(R\\).</p><p>If a path shares no stage line with another particle path or a hole, adding it to any subset multiplies that subset’s transition probability by \\(1/s\\). Its lines are independent of every other constraint, and each has the uniform one-slot marginal. This also holds when the subset has probability zero. The paired terms in (8) cancel. Thus \\(Q\\ne0\\) requires all \\(k\\) particles to be incident to line sharing.</p><p>Lemma 3.2 with \\(w=k\\) gives the probability bound. Lemma 3.1 then gives\n\\[\n\\|Q\\|_{\\mathrm{HS}}\n\\le\n2^{3k/2}e^{bh+(1+\\log4)bk}s^{-\\theta k/8}\n\\le s^{-\\theta k/16}.\n\\]\nThe last step uses \\(h\\le K_0k\\) and \\(b/\\log s\\le1/\\log R\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 3.4", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "\\( |s^kQ| \\le \\exp(O(b(k+h)))\\,\\eta^{|G|/2}. \\) The implicit constant is absolute.", "statement": "<p>Bounded-coordinate alternating entry estimate.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use the sparse setting of Proposition 3, but now assume \\(b\\le B_0=\\lceil8/\\theta\\rceil\\) and \\(z=0\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Put\n  \\[\n  \\xi=\\frac1{10B_0},\n  \\qquad \\eta=s^{-\\xi},\n  \\]\n  and take \\(R\\) large enough that \\(\\eta\\le1/2\\).</p><p class=\"li\">For an endpoint array, let \\(t_L\\) count its \\(k\\) auxiliary paths using line \\(L\\), whether or not those paths are jointly feasible.</p><p class=\"li\">A line is light if\n  \\[\n  h_L+t_L\\le |L|\\eta.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">A particle is good if every line on its path is light and it shares no layer slot with another particle or a hole. Let \\(G\\) be the set of good particles.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(Q\\) be the alternating kernel (8), with the zero extension of Lemma 3.1.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>\\[\n|s^kQ|\n\\le\n\\exp(O(b(k+h)))\\,\\eta^{|G|/2}. \\tag{11}\n\\]\nThe implicit constant is absolute.</p>", "proof": "<p>The assertion is immediate outside endpoint placements, where the extension is zero. For a subset \\(A\\) compatible with \\(\\mathcal H\\) at every layer, the uniform conditional line law gives\n\\[\ns^{|A|}p_A(x_A,y_A)\n=\n\\prod_L\\frac{m^{v_L(A)}}{(m-h_L)_{v_L(A)}},\n\\qquad m=|L|.\n\\]\nFor an invalid subset, its probability is zero and this product is not formed.</p><p>A good path shares no layer slot with another path or a hole. Thus \\(J\\cup S\\) is valid exactly when \\(J\\) is valid, for \\(J\\subseteq[k]\\setminus G\\) and \\(S\\subseteq G\\). Group the alternating sum by \\(J\\), discarding invalid \\(J\\) before forming line factors. For valid \\(J\\), let \\(r_L(J)\\) count its paths on \\(L\\), and put\n\\[\nD_J\n=\n\\prod_{L\\ \\mathrm{non\\text{-}light}}\n\\frac{m^{r_L(J)}}{(m-h_L)_{r_L(J)}}.\n\\]\nAll denominators are positive, and Proposition 2 gives\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\log D_J\n&amp;=\n\\sum_{L\\ \\mathrm{non\\text{-}light}}\n\\bigl(\\phi_{|L|}(h_L+r_L(J))-\\phi_{|L|}(h_L)\\bigr)\\\\\n&amp;\\le\n\\sum_L\\phi_{|L|}(h_L+r_L(J))\n\\le b(h+|J|).\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]</p><p>At each light line introduce \\(W_L=m/Z_L\\), where the \\(Z_L\\) are independent gamma variables of shape \\(m-h_L+1\\) and unit rate. The identity\n\\[\n\\mathbb EZ^{-v}\n=\\frac{\\Gamma(\\alpha-v)}{\\Gamma(\\alpha)},\n\\qquad\nZ\\sim\\operatorname{Gamma}(\\alpha,1),\\quad0\\le v&lt;\\alpha,\n\\]\nfollows by integrating the gamma density. It gives\n\\[\n\\mathbb EW_L^v=\\frac{m^v}{(m-h_L)_v}\n\\]\nfor all exponents used here. Lightness makes these moments finite.</p><p>If \\(L_j(i)\\) is the stage-\\(j\\) line of good path \\(i\\), alternation over good paths gives\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\ns^kQ(x,y)\n={}&amp;\n\\sum_{\\substack{J\\subseteq[k]\\setminus G\\\\J\\ \\mathrm{valid}}}\n(-1)^{k-|J|-|G|}D_J\\\\\n&amp;\\quad\\times\n\\mathbb E\\left[\n\\prod_{L\\ \\mathrm{light}}W_L^{r_L(J)}\n\\prod_{i\\in G}\n\\left(\\prod_{j=1}^bW_{L_j(i)}-1\\right)\n\\right].\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nEvery path using a light line uses the same \\(W_L\\). Group all factors by line before taking moments; independence is between lines, not between particle factors.</p><p>For nonnegative integers \\(u,v\\) with \\(u+v\\le t_L\\), density tilting gives\n\\[\n\\mathbb EW_L^v|W_L-1|^u\n=\n\\frac{m^{u+v}}{(m-h_L)_{u+v}}\\,\n\\mathbb E|1-Z'/m|^u\n\\le C_1^{u+v}\\eta^{u/2}, \\tag{12}\n\\]\nwhere \\(Z'\\) has gamma shape\n\\[\nn=m-h_L+1-u-v\n\\]\nand unit rate. Indeed,\n\\[\nn\\ge m(1-\\eta)+1&gt;0,\n\\qquad\n\\frac{m^{u+v}}{(m-h_L)_{u+v}}\n\\le(1-\\eta)^{-(u+v)}\n\\le2^{u+v}.\n\\]\nFor a gamma variable of shape \\(n\\), the centered moment generating function is \\(e^{-nt}(1-t)^{-n}\\), whose logarithm is at most \\(Cnt^2\\) for \\(|t|\\le1/2\\). Chernoff’s inequality gives\n\\[\n\\Pr(|Z'-n|&gt;x)\\le2\\exp[-c\\min\\{x^2/n,x\\}].\n\\]\nIntegrating this tail yields\n\\[\n(\\mathbb E|Z'-n|^u)^{1/u}\n\\le C(\\sqrt{nu}+u),\n\\qquad u\\ge1.\n\\]\nWhen \\(u\\ge1\\), lightness gives \\(u/m\\le\\eta\\), \\(n\\le m+1\\), and\n\\[\n|1-n/m|\\le\\eta+1/m\\le2\\eta.\n\\]\nHence\n\\[\n(\\mathbb E|1-Z'/m|^u)^{1/u}\n\\le\n|1-n/m|+\\frac C m(\\sqrt{nu}+u)\n\\le C'\\sqrt\\eta.\n\\]\nThis proves (12); for \\(u=0\\), only the prefactor bound is needed.</p><p>For each good path, telescope in stage order:\n\\[\n\\prod_{j=1}^bW_{L_j(i)}-1\n=\n\\sum_{a=1}^b\n(W_{L_a(i)}-1)\\prod_{j&lt;a}W_{L_j(i)}.\n\\]\nIn an expanded term, let \\(u_L\\) count its factors \\(W_L-1\\) and \\(v_L\\) its undifferenced factors \\(W_L\\). Every good path supplies one difference, so\n\\[\n\\sum_Lu_L=|G|.\n\\]\nA tracked path supplies at most one factor at any stage line, so \\(u_L+v_L\\le t_L\\). Independence across lines and (12) bound the expectation in absolute value by\n\\[\nC_1^{bk}\\eta^{|G|/2}.\n\\]\nThere are at most \\(b^{|G|}\\) telescoping choices and \\(2^{k-|G|}\\) choices of valid \\(J\\). Together with the bound on \\(D_J\\), these prove (11).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 3.5", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "\\( \\Pr_{\\mathrm{full}}(|G|<k/2)\\le s^{-\\nu k}, \\) and \\( \\|Q\\|_{\\mathrm{HS}} \\le \\exp(O(b(k+h))) \\left(s^{-\\xi k/4}+s^{-\\nu k/2}\\right). \\)", "statement": "<p>Counting good particles in bounded dimension.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use exactly the bounded-coordinate setting, \\(\\xi,\\eta,G,Q\\), and independent full-grid endpoint sampling of Lemma 3.4.</p><p class=\"li\">Keep the hole paths fixed.</p><p class=\"li\">Take \\(R\\) sufficiently large in terms of \\(\\theta,K_0\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Put\n  \\[\n  \\nu=\\frac{\\theta}{100B_0}&gt;0.\n  \\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>\\[\n\\Pr_{\\mathrm{full}}(|G|&lt;k/2)\\le s^{-\\nu k}, \\tag{13}\n\\]\nand\n\\[\n\\|Q\\|_{\\mathrm{HS}}\n\\le\n\\exp(O(b(k+h)))\n\\left(s^{-\\xi k/4}+s^{-\\nu k/2}\\right).\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>The sparse hypothesis and \\(\\xi\\le\\theta/80\\) give, for large \\(R\\),\n\\[\n\\frac{(b+1)(k+h)}s\\le s^{-\\theta/2},\n\\qquad\n\\frac{2h}{s\\eta},\\frac{2k}{s\\eta}\n\\le2s^{-(\\theta-\\xi)}\n\\le s^{-\\theta/2}.\n\\]\nIn particular these quantities are at most one.</p><p>If \\(|G|&lt;k/2\\), either at least \\(k/4\\) particles share a layer slot with another path, or at least \\(k/4\\) particles use a non-light line. Lemma 3.2 bounds the first event with \\(\\omega=(b+1)/s\\).</p><p>In the second event, some stage \\(j\\) has at least \\(k/(4B_0)\\) particles on non-light lines. Every such line satisfies\n\\[\nh_L&gt;m_j\\eta/2\n\\quad\\text{or}\\quad\nt_L&gt;m_j\\eta/2,\n\\]\nso one class receives at least \\(a_k=k/(8B_0)\\) particles.</p><p>At a fixed stage, the lines with \\(h_L&gt;m_j\\eta/2\\) are determined by the holes and occupy at most the fraction \\(2h/(s\\eta)\\) of all slots. Independent occupancy bounds the probability of at least \\(a_k\\) particles there by\n\\[\n2^k\\left(\\frac{2h}{s\\eta}\\right)^{a_k}.\n\\]</p><p>The number of lines with \\(t_L&gt;m_j\\eta/2\\) is at most\n\\[\nr_j=\\left\\lfloor\\frac{2k}{m_j\\eta}\\right\\rfloor.\n\\]\nIf \\(r_j=0\\), there are none. Otherwise fix at most \\(r_j\\) lines first. A particle uses them with probability at most\n\\[\nr_jm_j/s\\le2k/(s\\eta).\n\\]\nThe occupancy bound is\n\\[\n2^k\\left(\\frac{2k}{s\\eta}\\right)^{a_k},\n\\]\nand there are at most \\((s+1)^{r_j}\\) possible fixed sets. Therefore\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\Pr_{\\mathrm{full}}(|G|&lt;k/2)\n\\le{}&amp;\n2^k\\left(\\frac{(b+1)(k+h)}s\\right)^{k/8}\\\\\n&amp;+b2^k\\left(\\frac{2h}{s\\eta}\\right)^{a_k}\\\\\n&amp;+2^k\n\\sum_{\\substack{1\\le j\\le b\\\\r_j\\ge1}}\n(s+1)^{r_j}\n\\left(\\frac{2k}{s\\eta}\\right)^{a_k}.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nTaking ceilings of nonintegral thresholds only decreases powers of bases in \\([0,1]\\).</p><p>The set-selection cost is uniformly small. Since \\(b\\le B_0\\) and \\(R\\le m_j\\le R^2\\),\n\\[\nm_j\\ge s^{1/(2B_0)},\n\\qquad\nm_j\\eta\\ge s^{2/(5B_0)},\n\\]\nand\n\\[\n\\frac{r_j\\log(s+1)}{k\\log s}\n\\le\n2s^{-2/(5B_0)}\n\\frac{\\log(s+1)}{\\log s}\n=o_R(1).\n\\]\nBefore the factors \\(2^k\\), the stage sum, and this set count, the slot-sharing term saves \\((\\theta/16)k\\log s\\), and each non-light-line term saves \\((\\theta/(16B_0))k\\log s\\). The other costs are \\(o_R(k\\log s)\\). Increasing \\(R\\) gives (13).</p><p>On \\(|G|\\ge k/2\\), Lemma 3.4 gives the entry saving \\(s^{-\\xi k/4}\\). On the complement, use (9) and the square root of (13) in the Hilbert–Schmidt expectation of Lemma 3.1. This proves the asserted norm bound.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 3.6", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "The conditional average in the sign twist of the \\(k\\)-placement representation is zero.", "statement": "<p>Column types.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use the sparse setting of Proposition 3 with \\(\\lambda'_1=M-k\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Put \\(B_0=\\lceil8/\\theta\\rceil\\), and take \\(R\\) sufficiently large.</p><p class=\"li\">Assume either:</p><p class=\"li l2 num\">1. \\(b&gt;B_0\\) and \\(0\\le z\\le z_{\\mathrm{cmp}}(R)\\); or</p><p class=\"li l2 num\">2. \\(b\\le B_0\\) and \\(z=0\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The conditional average in the sign twist of the \\(k\\)-placement representation is zero. In particular,\n\\[\nK_\\lambda^{\\mathcal H}=0.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>The sign twist of the \\(k\\)-placement representation contains \\([\\lambda]\\). Its entry for placements \\(x,y\\) is\n\\[\n\\mathbb E\\bigl[\\operatorname{sgn}(g)\\mathbf1_{\\{g(x)=y\\}}\\mid\\mathcal H\\bigr],\n\\]\nwhere \\(g\\) is the remaining bijection in fixed slot identifications. Invalid full path families give zero entries. For a valid entry, condition on all \\(h+k\\) paths.</p><p>On each line, list the assigned paths first in the same order at input and output, followed by unassigned sites. Deleting the assignments leaves a residual bijection; comparison with the original slot orders contributes only a deterministic sign. Composing stages expresses the sign of the remaining bijection as a fixed factor times the product of residual line signs. The conditioned lines are independent, so it suffices to find one residual line sign with mean zero.</p><p>For \\(b&gt;B_0\\),\n\\[\n\\frac{s}{\\max_jm_j}\n\\ge s^{1-2/b}\n&gt;s^{1-\\theta}\n\\ge h+k.\n\\]\nEvery stage has more lines than paths, so one line is untouched. Its law \\(L_m(z)\\) has expected sign zero.</p><p>For \\(b\\le B_0\\) at \\(z=0\\), fix any stage \\(j\\). Once \\(R^{-\\theta}+R^{-1}&lt;1\\),\n\\[\ns-h-k\\ge s(1-s^{-\\theta})&gt;\\frac{s}{m_j}.\n\\]\nSince there are \\(s/m_j\\) lines, one has at least two unassigned sites. Its uniform residual bijection has expected sign zero. Thus every signed placement entry vanishes.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 4", "orig": "Lemma 5.1 (Signed density domination)", "depth": 0, "snippet": "The types present are precisely the \\((p,p)\\)-hook diagrams.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(s\\ge1\\), \\(1\\le p\\le s\\), and \\(0\\le l\\le s\\) be integers.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(V=E\\oplus O\\), where \\(\\dim E=\\dim O=p\\).</p><p class=\"li\">On \\(V^{\\otimes l}\\), let \\(S_l\\) act by permuting slots with signs: interchanging two neighboring odd factors incurs a minus sign; other neighboring interchanges do not.</p><p class=\"li\">An even density is a positive semidefinite operator on \\(V\\) of trace one that is block diagonal for \\(E\\oplus O\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(P_\\alpha\\) be the isotypic projection for \\(\\alpha\\vdash l\\), and put \\(F(\\alpha)=\\log D_\\alpha\\).</p><p class=\"li\">A \\((p,p)\\)-hook diagram has all boxes in its first \\(p\\) rows or first \\(p\\) columns.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The types present are precisely the \\((p,p)\\)-hook diagrams. For every such \\(\\alpha\\), there is a probability distribution \\(\\mu_\\alpha\\) on even densities such that, in positive semidefinite order,\n\\[\nP_\\alpha\n\\preceq\n\\exp\\{F(\\alpha)+O(p^2\\log(s+1))\\}\n\\,\\mathbb E_{r\\sim\\mu_\\alpha}r^{\\otimes l}. \\tag{14}\n\\]\nIts multiplicity is at most \\(\\exp(O(p^2\\log(s+1)))\\), and the number of types present is at most \\((s+1)^{2p}\\). All constants are absolute.</p><p>For a partition of sites into blocks, signed unitary reordering makes the product-subgroup action a tensor product. Applying (14) blockwise gives products of densities constant within each block. Undoing the signed reordering carries these to ordinary tensor products with relabeled sites. In particular, a constant tensor power of an even density commutes with the full signed symmetric-group action.</p>", "proof": "<p>For \\(l=0\\), the tensor space and its only representation have dimension one, and any density gives the assertion.</p><p>For \\(l&gt;0\\), Lemma 4.1 proves the hook support and bounds the number of types. The sector projections containing \\(\\alpha\\) cover \\(P_\\alpha\\). If their number is \\(N_\\alpha\\), then \\(N_\\alpha\\le(s+1)^{2p}\\). Sum the domination in Lemma 4.2 over those sectors, and choose a sector uniformly before choosing its Haar unitaries. The resulting distribution \\(\\mu_\\alpha\\) satisfies\n\\[\n\\sum_{\\text{relevant sectors}}\\mathbb E_Ur_U^{\\otimes l}\n=\nN_\\alpha\\mathbb E_{r\\sim\\mu_\\alpha}r^{\\otimes l}.\n\\]\nAbsorb \\(N_\\alpha\\) into the \\(O(p^2\\log(s+1))\\) term to obtain (14).</p><p>Taking traces gives the multiplicity bound, since\n\\[\n\\operatorname{Tr}P_\\alpha=D_\\alpha\\operatorname{mult}(\\alpha)\n\\]\nand every tensor density has trace one.</p><p>For disjoint blocks, signed unitary reordering makes the product-subgroup action a tensor product. Each density is even, so undoing that reordering gives the ordinary tensor product with relabeled sites. A constant tensor power of an even density consequently commutes with the full signed action.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 4.1", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "These are orthogonal sectors for the commuting actions of \\(S_l\\) and \\(U(E)\\times U(O)\\).", "statement": "<p>Parity sectors, hook support, and sector weight.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use the signed tensor representation in Proposition 4, with \\(l&gt;0\\).</p><p class=\"li\">For a fixed number \\(t\\) of even factors, let \\(\\sigma\\vdash t\\) and \\(\\tau\\vdash l-t\\) have at most \\(p\\) rows.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(W_\\sigma(E)\\) and \\(W_\\tau(O)\\) be the ordinary Schur–Weyl multiplicity spaces.</p><p class=\"li\">Define\n  \\[\n  \\mathcal V_{\\sigma,\\tau}\n  \\cong\n  \\operatorname{Ind}_{S_t\\times S_{l-t}}^{S_l}\n  ([\\sigma]\\boxtimes[\\tau'])\n  \\otimes W_\\sigma(E)\\otimes W_\\tau(O),\n  \\]\n  and\n  \\[\n  S=l\\log l-\\sum_i\\sigma_i\\log\\sigma_i-\\sum_j\\tau_j\\log\\tau_j,\n  \\]\n  with zero summands interpreted as zero.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>These are orthogonal sectors for the commuting actions of \\(S_l\\) and \\(U(E)\\times U(O)\\). Their symmetric-group types are precisely the \\((p,p)\\)-hook types.</p><p>If \\(\\alpha\\) occurs in \\(\\mathcal V_{\\sigma,\\tau}\\), then, padding partitions by zeros,\n\\[\n\\alpha_i\\le\\sigma_i+p,\n\\qquad\n\\alpha'_j\\le\\tau_j+p,\n\\]\nand\n\\[\nS\\le F(\\alpha)+O(p^2\\log(s+1)). \\tag{15}\n\\]\nThere are at most \\((s+1)^{2p}\\) hook types and at most \\((s+1)^{2p}\\) sectors.</p>", "proof": "<p>Fixing a parity pattern gives ordinary tensor powers of \\(E\\) and \\(O\\), with the latter symmetric-group action twisted by sign. Summing over parity patterns induces from \\(S_t\\times S_{l-t}\\), giving the displayed sector decomposition by ordinary Schur–Weyl duality.</p><p>The representation \\([\\tau']\\) occurs upon inducing sign representations on subgroups of sizes \\(\\tau_1,\\ldots,\\tau_p\\), since its columns can be added as vertical strips. Thus, if \\(\\alpha\\) occurs in the sector, Pieri’s rule reaches it from \\(\\sigma\\) by at most \\(p\\) vertical strips. Transposing and interchanging the factors gives the analogous assertion from \\(\\tau\\). Therefore\n\\[\n\\alpha_i\\le\\sigma_i+p,\n\\qquad\n\\alpha'_j\\le\\tau_j+p.\n\\]\nIn particular \\(\\alpha_{p+1}\\le p\\).</p><p>Conversely, take the first \\(p\\) rows of a hook shape as the even shape, and add the boxes below those rows by columns from left to right. These are at most \\(p\\) vertical strips. In the odd tensor power, words with one basis symbol for each strip and its prescribed multiplicity carry the corresponding induction of sign representations. Pieri’s rule supplies the hook shape.</p><p>The first \\(p\\) row lengths and first \\(p\\) column lengths specify a hook diagram, giving at most \\((s+1)^{2p}\\) possibilities. The padded pair \\((\\sigma,\\tau)\\) is also specified by \\(2p\\) integers between zero and \\(s\\).</p><p>For the weight estimate, put\n\\[\nH_i=(\\alpha_i-p)_+,\\qquad\nV_j=(\\alpha'_j-p)_+,\n\\qquad i,j\\le p.\n\\]\nThese are the horizontal and vertical arm lengths outside the \\(p\\)-by-\\(p\\) core. The strip inequalities give \\(H_i\\le\\sigma_i\\), \\(V_j\\le\\tau_j\\).</p><p>Each hook in a horizontal arm is at most its row hook plus \\(p\\). An arm of length \\(H_i\\) therefore has hook product at most\n\\[\n\\prod_{r=1}^{H_i}(r+p)\n=\nH_i!\\binom{H_i+p}{p}\n\\le H_i!(s+p)^p.\n\\]\nThe same holds for vertical arms, and the at most \\(p^2\\) core hooks have length at most \\(s\\). Hence\n\\[\nD_\\alpha\n\\ge\n(s+p)^{-O(p^2)}\n\\frac{l!}{\\prod_i\\sigma_i!\\prod_j\\tau_j!}.\n\\]\nUsing\n\\[\n\\log(n!)=n\\log n-n+O(\\log(n+1))\n\\]\nand \\(|\\sigma|+|\\tau|=l\\), the linear terms cancel and give (15).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 4.2", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "The matrix \\(r_U\\) is an even density, and \\( Q_{\\sigma,\\tau} \\preceq \\exp\\{F(\\alpha)+O(p^2\\log(s+1))\\} \\,\\mathbb E_Ur_U^{\\otimes l}. \\)", "statement": "<p>Positive density for a Schur–Weyl sector.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use a sector \\(\\mathcal V_{\\sigma,\\tau}\\) from Lemma 4.1, with \\(l&gt;0\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(Q_{\\sigma,\\tau}\\) be its orthogonal projection, and suppose \\(\\alpha\\) occurs in it.</p><p class=\"li\">Put\n  \\[\n  d_E=\\operatorname{diag}(\\sigma_1/l,\\ldots,\\sigma_p/l),\n  \\qquad\n  d_O=\\operatorname{diag}(\\tau_1/l,\\ldots,\\tau_p/l).\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">For independent Haar unitaries \\(U_E,U_O\\), let\n  \\[\n  r_U=(U_Ed_EU_E^*)\\oplus(U_Od_OU_O^*).\n  \\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The matrix \\(r_U\\) is an even density, and\n\\[\nQ_{\\sigma,\\tau}\n\\preceq\n\\exp\\{F(\\alpha)+O(p^2\\log(s+1))\\}\n\\,\\mathbb E_Ur_U^{\\otimes l}.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Write\n\\[\nm_\\sigma=\\dim W_\\sigma(E),\n\\qquad\nm_\\tau=\\dim W_\\tau(O).\n\\]\nThe Weyl dimension formula gives\n\\[\nm_\\sigma\n=\n\\prod_{1\\le i&lt;j\\le p}\n\\frac{\\sigma_i-\\sigma_j+j-i}{j-i}\n\\le(t+1)^{p(p-1)/2},\n\\]\nand similarly for \\(m_\\tau\\), replacing \\(t\\) by \\(l-t\\).</p><p>The tensor power of \\(d_E\\) acts on the multiplicity space as \\(W_\\sigma(d_E)\\). Its highest-weight line has eigenvalue\n\\[\n\\prod_i(\\sigma_i/l)^{\\sigma_i},\n\\]\nand all other eigenvalues are nonnegative. The analogous statement holds for \\(d_O\\). These assertions extend to zero eigenvalues by continuity, with \\(0^0=1\\).</p><p>The tensor density preserves every sector. Averaging over the two unitary groups makes its action scalar on the two irreducible multiplicity spaces. On the whole sector this scalar is\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{\\operatorname{Tr}W_\\sigma(d_E)}{m_\\sigma}\n\\frac{\\operatorname{Tr}W_\\tau(d_O)}{m_\\tau}\n&amp;\\ge\n\\frac{\\prod_i(\\sigma_i/l)^{\\sigma_i}\n      \\prod_j(\\tau_j/l)^{\\tau_j}}\n     {m_\\sigma m_\\tau}\\\\\n&amp;=\n\\frac{e^{-S}}{m_\\sigma m_\\tau}\n\\ge e^{-S-O(p^2\\log(s+1))}.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nThe same scalar acts on every parity pattern, so their number does not enter it. Positivity and (15) now give the asserted domination.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 5", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "For every \\((p,p)\\)-hook diagram \\(\\gamma\\vdash s-h'\\), \\( \\log T_\\gamma^{\\mathcal H'} \\le -\\widehat cF(\\gamma) +\\widehat e\\,h'\\log s -C(\\mathcal H') +s^{9/10}. \\) There is no lower bound on \\(F(\\gamma)\\).", "statement": "<p>The current-grid hook estimate: the unnumbered claim displayed as equation (18) in Section 6.1.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Put\n  \\[\n  a=\\frac1{100},\n  \\qquad c_0=e_0=\\frac a{100},\n  \\qquad\n  c(x)=c_0+\\frac1{\\sqrt{\\log x}},\n  \\qquad\n  e(x)=e_0-\\frac1{\\sqrt{\\log x}}.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Fix a positive integer \\(q\\), and let \\(R\\) be a sufficiently large power of two.</p><p class=\"li\">Consider an ordered grid with \\(b\\ge2\\), coordinate sizes powers of two in \\([R,R^2]\\), and total size \\(s\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Use independent line laws \\(L_m(z)=(1-z)U_m+zB_m\\), with \\(0\\le z\\le z_{\\mathrm{cmp}}(R)\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(\\mathcal H'\\) be any family of \\(h'\\) prescribed paths satisfying the coordinate rule and distinctness at every layer.</p><p class=\"li\">For any prescribed family \\(\\mathcal J\\), write\n  \\[\n  C(\\mathcal J)=\\sum_L\\log\\frac{|L|^{|\\mathcal J|_L}}{(|L|)_{|\\mathcal J|_L}},\n  \\qquad\n  T_\\tau^{\\mathcal J}\n  =\\|K_\\tau^{\\mathcal J}\\|_{2q}^{2q}.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Split the coordinate list into consecutive blocks of \\(\\lfloor b/2\\rfloor\\) and \\(\\lceil b/2\\rceil\\) coordinates, with products \\(s_1,s_2\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Assume the following child estimates: for each row subgrid of size \\(s_1\\) and each column subgrid of size \\(s_2\\), every admitted prescribed family \\(\\mathcal J\\), and every remaining type \\(\\tau\\),\n  \\[\n  \\log T_\\tau^{\\mathcal J}\n  \\le\n  -c(s_u)F(\\tau)+e(s_u)|\\mathcal J|\\log s_u-C(\\mathcal J),\n  \\]\n  where \\(u=1\\) for rows and \\(u=2\\) for columns.</p><p class=\"li\">Put\n  \\[\n  p=\\lfloor s^a\\rfloor,\n  \\qquad\n  \\widehat c=c(s)+\\frac1{20\\sqrt{\\log s}},\n  \\qquad\n  \\widehat e=e(s)-\\frac1{20\\sqrt{\\log s}}.\n  \\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For every \\((p,p)\\)-hook diagram \\(\\gamma\\vdash s-h'\\),\n\\[\n\\log T_\\gamma^{\\mathcal H'}\n\\le\n-\\widehat cF(\\gamma)\n+\\widehat e\\,h'\\log s\n-C(\\mathcal H')\n+s^{9/10}. \\tag{18}\n\\]\nThere is no lower bound on \\(F(\\gamma)\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Every coordinate contributes between \\(\\log R\\) and \\(2\\log R\\), so the split gives\n\\[\n\\frac15\\log s\\le\\log s_u\\le\\frac45\\log s,\n\\qquad u=1,2.\n\\]\nIn particular \\(p\\le s_u\\).</p><p>Let \\(W\\) denote the right side of (21), including its error. Terms with zero middle factor in (19) contribute nothing. A nonzero middle factor has norm at most one and satisfies (21). Since \\(c_*\\le2c_0&lt;1\\le q\\),\n\\[\nq\\log\\|P_\\beta^CP_\\gamma P_\\alpha^R\\|_{\\mathrm{op}}^2\n\\le\nc_*\\min(0,W)\n\\le c_*W.\n\\]\nAdding this to (20) cancels \\(F_R+F_C\\). The number of lists of local hook types contributes only\n\\[\nO_q((s_1+s_2)p^2\\log(s+1))\n\\]\nwhen the sum in (19) is raised to \\(2q\\). Hence\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\log T_\\gamma^{\\mathcal H'}\n\\le{}&amp;\n-c_*F(\\gamma)+e_*h'\\log s-C(\\mathcal H')\n+c_*h'\\\\\n&amp;+O_q((s_1+s_2)p^2\\log(s+1)).\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]</p><p>With \\(L=\\log s\\), the size split yields\n\\[\nc_*-c(s)=e(s)-e_*\n\\ge\n\\frac{\\sqrt5/2-1}{\\sqrt L}\n&gt;\n\\frac1{10\\sqrt L}.\n\\]\nThe hatted coefficients use only \\(1/(20\\sqrt L)\\) of each reserve. The remaining hole reserve is at least \\(h'\\sqrt L/20\\), which absorbs \\(c_*h'\\) for large \\(R\\). Also,\n\\[\n(s_1+s_2)p^2\\log(s+1)\n\\le\n2s^{4/5+2a}\\log(s+1)\n=\n2s^{0.82}\\log(s+1).\n\\]\nFor fixed \\(q\\), its \\(O_q\\) multiple is at most \\(s^{9/10}\\) once \\(R\\) is sufficiently large. These comparisons prove (18), including for hook types of small dimension.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 5.1", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "The row and column randomizations are conditionally independent, and …", "statement": "<p>Subgroup decomposition and child moments.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use the setting and child hypotheses of Proposition 5.</p><p class=\"li\">Regard the grid as \\(s_2\\) rows of length \\(s_1\\). The first coordinate block acts within rows and the second within columns.</p><p class=\"li\">At their junction, remove the middle slots of \\(\\mathcal H'\\), leaving \\(s-h'\\) free sites.</p><p class=\"li\">Use the signed tensor representation on those free sites with \\(V=E\\oplus O\\), \\(\\dim E=\\dim O=p\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(X\\) be the conditional row average from input to junction and \\(Y\\) the conditional column average from junction to output.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(P_\\gamma\\) be the full-group isotypic projection at the junction. Let \\(P_\\alpha^R\\) and \\(P_\\beta^C\\) be the row and column subgroup isotypic projections, where \\(\\alpha_i\\) and \\(\\beta_j\\) partition the numbers of free sites in their row and column.</p><p class=\"li\">Put\n  \\[\n  F_R=\\sum_iF(\\alpha_i),\n  \\qquad\n  F_C=\\sum_jF(\\beta_j),\n  \\]\n  \\[\n  c_*=\\min_{u=1,2}c(s_u),\n  \\qquad\n  e_*=\\max_{u=1,2}e(s_u).\n  \\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The row and column randomizations are conditionally independent, and\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n(T_\\gamma^{\\mathcal H'})^{1/(2q)}\n&amp;\\le\\|YP_\\gamma X\\|_{2q}\\\\\n&amp;\\le\n\\sum_{\\alpha,\\beta}\n\\|YP_\\beta^C\\|_{2q}\n\\|P_\\beta^CP_\\gamma P_\\alpha^R\\|_{\\mathrm{op}}\n\\|P_\\alpha^RX\\|_{2q}. \\qquad(19)\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nMoreover,\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\log\\left(\n\\|P_\\alpha^RX\\|_{2q}^{2q}\n\\|YP_\\beta^C\\|_{2q}^{2q}\n\\right)\n\\le{}&amp;\n-c_*(F_R+F_C)\n+e_*h'\\log s\n-C(\\mathcal H')\\\\\n&amp;+O((s_1+s_2)p^2\\log(s+1)). \\qquad(20)\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Splitting each prescribed path at the junction gives the row and column path constraints. Conditional independence of the stage lines gives the independence of \\(X\\) and \\(Y\\), so \\(YX\\) is the full average.</p><p>The full projection commutes with the subgroup projections. Bijections between layers intertwine the full isotypic projections. Under compatible unitary identifications, the restriction of \\(YP_\\gamma X\\) between the \\(\\gamma\\)-isotypic spaces is\n\\[\nK_\\gamma^{\\mathcal H'}\\otimes I\n\\]\non the tensor multiplicity space. Proposition 4 ensures this multiplicity is at least one. Inserting subgroup decompositions gives (19), using the triangle inequality, the product norm inequality, and \\(\\|A\\|_{\\mathrm{op}}\\le\\|A\\|_{2q}\\) for an outer factor.</p><p>Order free sites by rows at both ends of the first block. Signed unitary reordering gives \\(X=\\bigotimes_iX_i\\). If \\(\\mathcal H_i\\) is the path family in row \\(i\\), then\n\\[\n\\|P_\\alpha^RX\\|_{2q}^{2q}\n=\n\\prod_i\\operatorname{mult}(\\alpha_i)\\,T_{\\alpha_i}^{\\mathcal H_i}.\n\\]\nThere is an identical column formula. Proposition 4 bounds each multiplicity by \\(\\exp(O(p^2\\log(s+1)))\\).</p><p>Apply the child estimate to each local constrained sweep. Every stage line belongs to exactly one child subproblem, so their potentials add to \\(C(\\mathcal H')\\). Each coordinate block has \\(h'\\) prescribed paths, and\n\\[\n\\log s_1+\\log s_2=\\log s.\n\\]\nReplacing local coefficients by \\(c_*,e_*\\) gives (20).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 5.2", "orig": "Lemma 6.1 (Overlap at the junction)", "depth": 1, "snippet": "\\( \\log\\|P_\\beta^CP_\\gamma P_\\alpha^R\\|_{\\mathrm{op}}^2 \\le F_R+F_C-F(\\gamma)+h' +O((s_1+s_2)p^2\\log(s+1)). \\) A zero norm has logarithm \\(-\\infty\\), and the constant is absolute.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use the free junction board, signed tensor representation, type lists \\(\\alpha,\\beta\\), and full hook type \\(\\gamma\\) of Lemma 5.1.</p><p class=\"li\">There are \\(s_2\\) rows, \\(s_1\\) columns, and \\(h'\\) missing sites.</p><p class=\"li\">Put \\(F_R=\\sum_iF(\\alpha_i)\\) and \\(F_C=\\sum_jF(\\beta_j)\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>\\[\n\\log\\|P_\\beta^CP_\\gamma P_\\alpha^R\\|_{\\mathrm{op}}^2\n\\le\nF_R+F_C-F(\\gamma)+h'\n+O((s_1+s_2)p^2\\log(s+1)). \\tag{21}\n\\]\nA zero norm has logarithm \\(-\\infty\\), and the constant is absolute.</p>", "proof": "<p>Since \\(P_\\gamma\\) commutes with both subgroup projections,\n\\[\n\\|P_\\beta^CP_\\gamma P_\\alpha^R\\|_{\\mathrm{op}}^2\n\\le\n\\|P_\\beta^CP_\\gamma P_\\alpha^R\\|_{\\mathrm{HS}}^2\n=\n\\operatorname{Tr}(P_\\alpha^RP_\\beta^CP_\\gamma).\n\\]\nThe product \\(P_\\beta^CP_\\gamma\\) is a positive projection. Dominate \\(P_\\alpha^R\\) inside this trace using Proposition 4. Apart from\n\\[\n\\exp(F_R+O(s_2p^2\\log(s+1))),\n\\]\nthis leaves a probability mixture of product densities whose one-site matrix in row \\(i\\) is an even density \\(r_i\\).</p><p>For one such row family, average over all \\(s_2\\) rows, including empty ones. For \\(0&lt;\\varepsilon&lt;1\\), put\n\\[\n\\bar r=\\frac1{s_2}\\sum_ir_i,\n\\qquad\n\\bar r_\\varepsilon=(1-\\varepsilon)\\bar r+\\varepsilon I/\\dim V,\n\\qquad\nA_\\varepsilon=\\bar r_\\varepsilon^{-1/2}.\n\\]\nWith \\(n=s-h'\\), the positive operator\n\\[\nB_\\varepsilon=\\bar r_\\varepsilon^{\\otimes n}\n\\]\nhas trace one and commutes with the full signed group action. On the \\(\\gamma\\)-isotypic space it has form \\(I_{D_\\gamma}\\otimes B_\\gamma\\). Every eigenvalue there is counted at least \\(D_\\gamma\\) times in its trace, so\n\\[\nB_\\varepsilon P_\\gamma\\preceq D_\\gamma^{-1}P_\\gamma.\n\\]\nIt also commutes with \\(P_\\beta^C\\). Compress by that projection, use \\(P_\\beta^CP_\\gamma\\preceq P_\\beta^C\\) on the right, and conjugate by \\(B_\\varepsilon^{-1/2}\\). Thus\n\\[\nP_\\beta^CP_\\gamma\n\\preceq\nD_\\gamma^{-1}\nA_\\varepsilon^{\\otimes n}P_\\beta^CA_\\varepsilon^{\\otimes n}.\n\\]</p><p>Now dominate the column projection by its density mixture. Apart from\n\\[\n\\exp(F_C+O(s_1p^2\\log(s+1))),\n\\]\neach integrand is\n\\[\n\\prod_{(i,j)\\ \\mathrm{free}}\n\\operatorname{tr}(A_\\varepsilon r_iA_\\varepsilon v_j),\n\\]\nwhere \\(v_j\\) is the column density. Each factor is nonnegative, since it equals\n\\[\n\\|r_i^{1/2}A_\\varepsilon v_j^{1/2}\\|_{\\mathrm{HS}}^2.\n\\]\nFor every column,\n\\[\n\\sum_i\\operatorname{tr}(A_\\varepsilon r_iA_\\varepsilon v_j)\n=\ns_2\\operatorname{tr}(A_\\varepsilon\\bar rA_\\varepsilon v_j)\n\\le\\frac{s_2}{1-\\varepsilon}.\n\\]\nIf that column is missing \\(l&lt;s_2\\) sites, AM–GM bounds the product on retained sites by\n\\[\n(1-\\varepsilon)^{-(s_2-l)}\n\\left(\\frac{s_2}{s_2-l}\\right)^{s_2-l}\n\\le\n(1-\\varepsilon)^{-(s_2-l)}e^l.\n\\]\nFor \\(l=s_2\\), the empty product is one. There are \\(h'\\) missing sites in total. Multiply over columns, average the uniformly bounded integrands, and let \\(\\varepsilon\\downarrow0\\). This proves (21).</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 6", "orig": "Theorem 3.1 (Conditional sweep moment estimate)", "depth": 0, "snippet": "There are a power of two \\(R\\ge2\\), a positive integer \\(q\\), and \\(z_*>0\\) such that, for every such grid, path family, \\(z\\in[0,z_*]\\), and \\(\\lambda\\vdash s-h\\) …", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Consider ordered grids\n  \\[\n  \\Omega=\\Omega_1\\times\\cdots\\times\\Omega_b,\n  \\qquad b\\ge1,\n  \\qquad s=\\prod_jm_j,\n  \\]\n  with every \\(m_j=|\\Omega_j|\\) a power of two in \\([R,R^2]\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Use independent stage-line laws\n  \\[\n  L_m(z)=(1-z)U_m+zB_m.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(\\mathcal H\\) prescribe \\(h\\) coordinate-rule paths with distinct slots at every layer. After removing them, let \\(K_\\lambda^{\\mathcal H}\\) be the conditional average of the remaining bijection in \\([\\lambda]\\), where \\(\\lambda\\vdash s-h\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Define\n  \\[\n  F(\\lambda)=\\log D_\\lambda,\n  \\qquad\n  C(\\mathcal H)=\\sum_L\\phi_{|L|}(h_L),\n  \\qquad\n  \\phi_m(u)=\\log\\frac{m^u}{(m)_u},\n  \\]\n  and\n  \\[\n  T_\\lambda^{\\mathcal H}\n  =\\|K_\\lambda^{\\mathcal H}\\|_{2q}^{2q}.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Use\n  \\[\n  a=\\frac1{100},\n  \\qquad c_0=e_0=\\frac a{100},\n  \\qquad\n  c(s)=c_0+\\frac1{\\sqrt{\\log s}},\n  \\qquad\n  e(s)=e_0-\\frac1{\\sqrt{\\log s}}.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">A zero moment has logarithm \\(-\\infty\\). The empty diagram has dimension one; if \\(h=s\\), the remaining bijection is the unique empty bijection and its moment is one.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There are a power of two \\(R\\ge2\\), a positive integer \\(q\\), and \\(z_*&gt;0\\) such that, for every such grid, path family, \\(z\\in[0,z_*]\\), and \\(\\lambda\\vdash s-h\\),\n\\[\n\\log T_\\lambda^{\\mathcal H}\n\\le\n-c(s)F(\\lambda)+e(s)h\\log s-C(\\mathcal H). \\tag{5}\n\\]\nThis includes the one-dimensional trivial representation for every \\(h\\). In particular, for \\(\\mathcal H=\\varnothing\\),\n\\[\nT_\\lambda^\\varnothing\\le D_\\lambda^{-c(s)}.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Induct on the number \\(b\\) of coordinates, simultaneously for every allowed grid, feasible prescribed family, and irreducible representation.</p><p>Fix constants in the required order. Take \\(\\theta=a/16\\) and \\(K_0=10/e_0\\) in Proposition 3. Its exponent \\(\\rho\\) is uniform for sufficiently large \\(R\\). Choose\n\\[\nq\\ge\\max\\{1,\\lceil2/\\rho\\rceil\\},\n\\]\nand only then choose a sufficiently large power of two \\(R\\). Require all size bounds and error absorptions in Lemmas 6.1–6.3 and Proposition 5 uniformly for \\(s\\ge R\\). After fixing \\(R\\), choose \\(z_*&gt;0\\) small enough for (3), Proposition 3, and the base case below.</p><p>The one-dimensional cases and zero moments are covered by Lemma 6.1.</p><p>For \\(b=1\\) and \\(z=0\\), conditioning on the prescribed assignments leaves a uniform bijection on the remaining slots, so every nontrivial average is zero. For fixed \\(R\\), there are finitely many one-coordinate grids, path families, and representations; their conditional operators are continuous at zero. Decrease \\(z_*\\) to obtain the base case on a common interval.</p><p>Now let \\(b\\ge2\\), assuming the theorem for fewer coordinates. Lemma 6.1 settles the automatic cases and the range\n\\[\nF(\\lambda)&lt;s^{1-a/4}.\n\\]\nFor the remaining range (17), the induction hypothesis supplies the child bounds in Proposition 5. Lemma 6.2 records the additional path fibers and proves (24). Lemma 6.3 absorbs the added-path costs and proves (5).</p><p>All scale requirements hold after fixing a sufficiently large absolute \\(R\\) after \\(q\\). A final common positive \\(z_*\\) is obtained from the line comparison, sparse estimate, and finite base case. The simultaneous induction is complete.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 6.1", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "The estimate (5) holds whenever \\(F(\\lambda)=0\\), whenever \\( (1+c(s))F(\\lambda) \\le e(s)h\\log s-C(\\mathcal H), \\) and whenever \\( F(\\lambda)<s^{1-a/4}. \\)", "statement": "<p>Automatic and sparse-dimension cases of the moment estimate.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use the grids, laws, paths, moments, and coefficient functions in Proposition 6.</p><p class=\"li\">Take\n  \\[\n  \\theta=a/16,\n  \\qquad K_0=10/e_0.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(\\rho\\) be supplied by Proposition 3 for these parameters, and fix an integer\n  \\[\n  q\\ge\\max\\{1,\\lceil2/\\rho\\rceil\\},\n  \\qquad 2q\\rho\\ge4.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Take \\(R\\) sufficiently large that\n  \\[\n  \\frac1{\\sqrt{\\log R}}\\le\\frac{e_0}2,\n  \\qquad\n  \\frac1{\\log R}\\le\\frac{e_0}4,\n  \\]\n  as well as sufficiently large for Proposition 3.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(0\\le z\\le z_{\\mathrm{sp}}(R)\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The estimate (5) holds whenever \\(F(\\lambda)=0\\), whenever\n\\[\n(1+c(s))F(\\lambda)\n\\le e(s)h\\log s-C(\\mathcal H), \\tag{16}\n\\]\nand whenever\n\\[\nF(\\lambda)&lt;s^{1-a/4}.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Since \\(b\\le\\log s/\\log R\\) and \\(C(\\mathcal H)\\le bh\\), the choices give\n\\[\nc(s)\\le2c_0,\n\\qquad\ne(s)\\ge e_0/2,\n\\qquad\ne(s)h\\log s-C(\\mathcal H)\n\\ge(e_0/4)h\\log s.\n\\]\nEvery conditional average is a contraction. If \\(F(\\lambda)=0\\), its moment is at most one and the nonnegative allowance proves (5). This covers the empty diagram, \\(s-h=1\\), and all one-dimensional types. Zero moments satisfy the estimate by convention.</p><p>In general, contraction gives \\(T_\\lambda^{\\mathcal H}\\le D_\\lambda\\), so (16) implies (5).</p><p>Suppose now \\(F(\\lambda)&lt;s^{1-a/4}\\) and (16) fails. The positive allowance gives\n\\[\nh\\log s\\le K_0F(\\lambda),\n\\]\nand \\(M=s-h\\ge s/2\\) for large \\(R\\). Put\n\\[\nk=\\min(M-\\lambda_1,M-\\lambda'_1).\n\\]\nProposition 1 gives\n\\[\nc_{\\mathrm{dim}}k\\le F(\\lambda)\\le k\\log s.\n\\]\nHence \\(h\\le K_0k\\), and\n\\[\nh+k\\le s^{1-\\theta}\n\\]\nfor large \\(R\\), because \\(\\theta=a/16&lt;a/4\\). Also \\(k\\ge1\\), since \\(F(\\lambda)&gt;0\\).</p><p>Proposition 3 applies. There are \\(D_\\lambda\\) singular values, so\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\log T_\\lambda^{\\mathcal H}\n&amp;\\le F(\\lambda)-2q\\rho k\\log s\\\\\n&amp;\\le-3F(\\lambda)\\\\\n&amp;\\le-c(s)F(\\lambda)+e(s)h\\log s-C(\\mathcal H).\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 6.2", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "\\( F(\\lambda)\\le F(\\gamma)+k\\log s, \\) and \\( \\begin{aligned} \\log T_\\lambda^{\\mathcal H} \\le{}& -\\widehat cF(\\gamma) +\\widehat e(h+k)\\log s +s^{9/10} -C(\\mathcal H)\\\\ &+(\\log4)kb-F(\\delta). \\end{aligned} \\)", "statement": "<p>Adding boxes outside the hook.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use the fixed coefficient functions and sufficiently large \\(R\\), fixed \\(q\\), and grid laws of Proposition 6.</p><p class=\"li\">Let the current grid have \\(b\\ge2\\) coordinates, size \\(s\\), and prescribed family \\(\\mathcal H\\) of size \\(h\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Assume (5) for every admitted grid with fewer than \\(b\\) coordinates, every feasible prescribed family, and every remaining irreducible type.</p><p class=\"li\">Put \\(M=s-h\\), and let \\(\\lambda\\vdash M\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Set\n  \\[\n  p=\\lfloor s^a\\rfloor,\n  \\qquad\n  \\widehat c=c(s)+\\frac1{20\\sqrt{\\log s}},\n  \\qquad\n  \\widehat e=e(s)-\\frac1{20\\sqrt{\\log s}}.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Keep the \\((p,p)\\)-hook part \\(\\gamma\\) of \\(\\lambda\\). Translate the boxes outside that hook by subtracting \\(p\\) from both indices, obtaining an ordinary partition \\(\\delta\\), and put \\(k=|\\delta|\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>\\[\nF(\\lambda)\\le F(\\gamma)+k\\log s, \\tag{22}\n\\]\nand\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\log T_\\lambda^{\\mathcal H}\n\\le{}&amp;\n-\\widehat cF(\\gamma)\n+\\widehat e(h+k)\\log s\n+s^{9/10}\n-C(\\mathcal H)\\\\\n&amp;+(\\log4)kb-F(\\delta). \\qquad(24)\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>The skew shape \\(\\lambda/\\gamma\\) is a translate of \\(\\delta\\). Branching and Frobenius reciprocity give multiplicity \\(D_\\delta\\) for \\(\\lambda\\) in the representation induced from \\([\\gamma]\\) on \\(S_{M-k}\\) to \\(S_M\\). The induced space has dimension \\((M)_kD_\\gamma\\), giving (22).</p><p>Realize this space with one fiber of type \\([\\gamma]\\) for every placement of \\(k\\) distinguishable cards in the free slots. A bijection moves the placement and acts on its fiber by the bijection of remaining slots. Consistent slot identifications change fiber maps only by unitaries.</p><p>The average block from \\(x\\) to \\(y\\) is \\(p_{\\mathcal H}(x,y)\\) times the conditional operator in \\([\\gamma]\\) after the extra paths have been prescribed. A positive transition forces a feasible augmented family \\(\\mathcal H'\\) of \\(h+k\\) paths. The induction hypothesis supplies the child assumptions of Proposition 5, so (18) applies to every nonzero block, without a dimension condition on \\(\\gamma\\).</p><p>The induced average’s \\(2q\\)-moment is the sum of irreducible moments with multiplicities, and is at least \\(D_\\delta T_\\lambda^{\\mathcal H}\\). Expand its trace over cyclic placement indices with \\(2q\\) edges, alternating between input and output placement sets. Let \\(p_j\\) be the transition entry on edge \\(j\\), using the transposed transition matrix on an adjoint edge. The fiber map on an adjoint edge is the adjoint of its forward conditional operator; it has the same singular values and the same forward path potential.</p><p>For a nonzero cyclic term, let \\(\\mathcal H'_j\\) be its augmented family on edge \\(j\\). The triangle inequality and matrix trace Hölder give\n\\[\nD_\\delta T_\\lambda^{\\mathcal H}\n\\le\n\\sum\n\\left(\\prod_{j=1}^{2q}p_j\\right)\n\\prod_{j=1}^{2q}\n(T_\\gamma^{\\mathcal H'_j})^{1/(2q)}. \\tag{23}\n\\]\nThe sum may be restricted to nonzero cyclic terms.</p><p>Apply (18). Apart from \\(-C(\\mathcal H'_j)\\), all its terms depend only on \\(s,h+k,\\gamma\\). To treat the potentials, apply (4) to a \\(1/(2q)\\) power of each \\(p_j\\). Multiplication gives\n\\[\n\\prod_{j=1}^{2q}\n\\left(p_je^{-C(\\mathcal H'_j)/(2q)}\\right)\n\\le\ns^{-k}e^{-C(\\mathcal H)+(\\log4)kb}\n\\prod_{j=1}^{2q}p_j^{1-1/(2q)}.\n\\]\nThe augmented potentials disappear, leaving the original potential.</p><p>Put \\(n_k=(M)_k\\), and label the placement variables \\(x_1,\\ldots,x_{2q}\\) cyclically. Each edge matrix is the placement transition matrix or its transpose. Both row and column sums are one, since the transition averages bijections of free slots.</p><p>For each \\(r\\), let \\(A_r\\) be the product of all edge entries except the \\(r\\)th. Then\n\\[\n\\prod_{j=1}^{2q}p_j^{1-1/(2q)}\n=\n\\prod_{r=1}^{2q}A_r^{1/(2q)}.\n\\]\nDeleting an edge opens the cycle into a chain. Successive endpoint summation uses row or column sums equal to one, and the last placement has \\(n_k\\) values. Thus \\(\\sum A_r=n_k\\), and scalar Hölder gives\n\\[\n\\sum_{x_1,\\ldots,x_{2q}}\n\\prod_{j=1}^{2q}p_j^{1-1/(2q)}\n\\le\n\\prod_{r=1}^{2q}\\left(\\sum A_r\\right)^{1/(2q)}\n=n_k.\n\\]\nRestriction to nonzero cyclic terms only decreases the sum. The net counting factor is\n\\[\ns^{-k}(M)_k\\le1.\n\\]\nSubstitution into (23), followed by division by \\(D_\\delta\\), proves (24).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 6.3", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "The conditional moment bound (5) holds for \\(\\lambda\\).", "statement": "<p>Closing the large-dimension estimate.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use the grid, diagrams \\(\\lambda,\\gamma,\\delta\\), integer \\(k\\), coefficients \\(\\widehat c,\\widehat e\\), and sufficiently large \\(R\\) of Lemma 6.2.</p><p class=\"li\">Assume its estimates (22) and (24).</p><p class=\"li\">Assume\n  \\[\n  F(\\lambda)\\ge s^{1-a/4}. \\tag{17}\n  \\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The conditional moment bound (5) holds for \\(\\lambda\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Since \\(\\widehat c&gt;0\\), (22) and (24) give\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\log T_\\lambda^{\\mathcal H}\n\\le{}&amp;\n-\\widehat cF(\\lambda)\n+\\widehat e\\,h\\log s\n-C(\\mathcal H)\n+s^{9/10}\\\\\n&amp;+\\left[\n(\\widehat c+\\widehat e)k\\log s\n+(\\log4)kb\n-F(\\delta)\n\\right].\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nThe positive \\(k\\)-terms are the cost of changing the dimension term from \\(\\gamma\\) to \\(\\lambda\\) and prescribing the extra paths. Their logarithmic coefficient is\n\\[\n\\widehat c+\\widehat e=c_0+e_0=\\frac a{50}.\n\\]</p><p>Every row of \\(\\delta\\) is no longer than the \\((p+1)\\)st row of \\(\\lambda\\), and likewise for columns. Both lengths are at most \\(s/(p+1)\\). Thus every hook of \\(\\delta\\) has length at most \\(2s/(p+1)\\).</p><p>If \\(k\\ge s^{1-a/2}\\), the hook formula and \\(k!\\ge(k/e)^k\\) give\n\\[\nF(\\delta)\n\\ge\nk\\log\\frac{k(p+1)}{2es}\n\\ge\nk\\left(\\frac a2\\log s-\\log(2e)\\right).\n\\]\nFor large \\(R\\), this dominates the bracket’s positive terms, because \\(a/50&lt;a/2\\) and \\(b/\\log s\\le1/\\log R\\).</p><p>If \\(k&lt;s^{1-a/2}\\), discard \\(-F(\\delta)\\le0\\); the bracket is at most\n\\[\nO(s^{1-a/2}\\log s).\n\\]\nFinally, (17) gives\n\\[\n(\\widehat c-c(s))F(\\lambda)\n\\ge\n\\frac{s^{1-a/4}}{20\\sqrt{\\log s}},\n\\]\nwhile\n\\[\ns^{9/10}+s^{1-a/2}\\log s\n=\no\\left(\\frac{s^{1-a/4}}{\\sqrt{\\log s}}\\right).\n\\]\nFor sufficiently large \\(R\\), the dimension reserve absorbs all remaining errors. Since \\(\\widehat e&lt;e(s)\\), the old hole allowance also improves. This proves (5).</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 7", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "There is an absolute \\(g>0\\), independent of the grid, its number of coordinates, and \\(\\lambda\\), such that \\( \\|K_\\lambda(1)\\|_{\\mathrm{op}}\\le D_\\lambda^{-g}. \\)", "statement": "<p>Analytic transfer to binary grid sweeps, from Section 7.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Fix the absolute constants \\(R,q,z_*\\) supplied by Proposition 6.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(\\Omega\\) be any ordered grid whose coordinate sizes are powers of two in \\([R,R^2]\\), with total size \\(s\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Prescribe no paths.</p><p class=\"li\">For \\(\\lambda\\vdash s\\), let \\(K_\\lambda(z)\\) be the sweep average with line laws \\((1-z)U_m+zB_m\\). For complex \\(z\\), this notation denotes its unconditioned matrix polynomial.</p><p class=\"li\">Put \\(D_\\lambda=\\dim[\\lambda]\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There is an absolute \\(g&gt;0\\), independent of the grid, its number of coordinates, and \\(\\lambda\\), such that\n\\[\n\\|K_\\lambda(1)\\|_{\\mathrm{op}}\\le D_\\lambda^{-g}. \\tag{25}\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Put \\(a_0=c_0/(2q)&gt;0\\). Proposition 6 with no paths gives\n\\[\n\\|K_\\lambda(z)\\|_{\\mathrm{op}}\n\\le\n\\|K_\\lambda(z)\\|_{2q}\n\\le D_\\lambda^{-a_0},\n\\qquad 0\\le z\\le z_*.\n\\]\nLemma 7.1 supplies an absolute \\(r&gt;1\\) on whose closed disk all these operators are contractive.</p><p>Choose\n\\[\n0&lt;u&lt;v&lt;\\min\\{z_*,1,r-1\\},\n\\]\nand construct \\(G\\) as in Lemma 7.2. For fixed unit vectors \\(u_0,v_0\\), let\n\\[\nf(z)=u_0^*K_\\lambda(z)v_0,\n\\qquad\nA=a_0\\log D_\\lambda.\n\\]\nThis scalar polynomial satisfies the disk and segment bounds of Lemma 7.2. Hence\n\\[\n|u_0^*K_\\lambda(1)v_0|\n\\le D_\\lambda^{-a_0G(1)}.\n\\]\nTaking the supremum over the unit vectors gives (25) with\n\\[\ng=a_0G(1)&gt;0.\n\\]</p><p>The choices of \\(\\kappa,r,u,v,\\varepsilon_0\\) depend only on the fixed constants \\(R,q,z_*\\). Thus \\(g\\) is independent of the grid and representation.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 7.1", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "\\(\\kappa<1\\).", "statement": "<p>A contractive complex disk.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use the finitely many allowed line sizes determined by the fixed \\(R\\) in Proposition 7.</p><p class=\"li\">For each such \\(m\\), average the binary sweep \\(B_m\\) in every nontrivial irreducible representation of \\(S_m\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(\\kappa\\) be the maximum of the resulting operator norms.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>\\(\\kappa&lt;1\\). If \\(\\kappa=0\\), choose \\(r=2\\); otherwise choose \\(1&lt;r&lt;\\kappa^{-1}\\). Then for every grid and representation in Proposition 7,\n\\[\n\\|K_\\lambda(z)\\|_{\\mathrm{op}}\\le1\n\\qquad (|z|\\le r).\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Averaging by \\(B_m\\) is a product of orthogonal projections, one for each independent-switch layer. If the product had norm one, finite dimensionality would give a unit vector attaining it. Equality through successive projections would force that vector to be fixed by every switch group. Those groups contain all cube-edge transpositions and hence generate \\(S_m\\). Thus the norm is strictly less than one in every nontrivial irreducible. There are finitely many allowed sizes and types, so \\(\\kappa&lt;1\\).</p><p>Restrict \\([\\lambda]\\) to a line subgroup \\(S_m\\), and let \\(P,Q\\) be the averages of \\(U_m,B_m\\). Uniform averaging gives\n\\[\nPQ=QP=P.\n\\]\nRelative to \\(\\operatorname{ran}P\\oplus\\ker P\\),\n\\[\n(1-z)P+zQ\n=\nI_{\\operatorname{ran}P}\\oplus zQ|_{\\ker P},\n\\]\nand therefore\n\\[\n\\|(1-z)P+zQ\\|_{\\mathrm{op}}\n\\le\\max\\{1,|z|\\kappa\\}.\n\\]\nIndeed \\(Q|_{\\ker P}\\) is a direct sum of nontrivial line averages with arbitrary multiplicities.</p><p>Every line average is contractive on \\(|z|\\le r\\). Independence makes a stage average the product of its commuting line averages, and the full sweep average is the ordered product of stage averages. Submultiplicativity gives the conclusion.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 7.2", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "The choice of \\(\\varepsilon_0\\) can be made so that \\(G\\le1\\) on \\([u,v]\\), \\(G\\le0\\) on \\(|z|=r\\), and \\(G(1)>0\\).", "statement": "<p>Slit-disk subharmonic transfer.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(r&gt;1\\) and \\(0&lt;u&lt;v&lt;\\min\\{1,r-1\\}\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Put\n  \\[\n  \\mathcal D=\\{z\\in\\mathbb C:|z|&lt;r\\}\\setminus[u,v].\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Choose \\(\\varepsilon_0&gt;0\\) sufficiently small and define\n  \\[\n  G(z)=\\varepsilon_0\\int_u^v\\log\\frac1{|z-t|}\\,dt.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(A\\ge0\\), and let \\(f\\) be a scalar polynomial satisfying\n  \\[\n  |f(z)|\\le1\\quad(|z|\\le r),\n  \\qquad\n  |f(t)|\\le e^{-A}\\quad(t\\in[u,v]).\n  \\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The choice of \\(\\varepsilon_0\\) can be made so that \\(G\\le1\\) on \\([u,v]\\), \\(G\\le0\\) on \\(|z|=r\\), and \\(G(1)&gt;0\\). For this choice,\n\\[\n|f(1)|\\le e^{-AG(1)}.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>The function \\(G\\) is harmonic off the segment. Direct integration of the logarithm gives a continuous extension across the segment, including its endpoints. On \\(|z|=r\\),\n\\[\n|z-t|\\ge r-v&gt;1,\n\\]\nso \\(G(z)\\le0\\). Its values on the closed segment are bounded, so choose \\(\\varepsilon_0&gt;0\\) small enough that \\(G\\le1\\) there. Also \\(G(1)&gt;0\\), since \\(0&lt;1-t&lt;1\\) throughout \\([u,v]\\).</p><p>The case \\(f\\equiv0\\) is immediate. Otherwise \\(\\log|f|\\) is subharmonic, with value \\(-\\infty\\) at zeros. Thus\n\\[\nH=\\log|f|+AG\n\\]\nis subharmonic on \\(\\mathcal D\\). The assumed bounds and continuity at the slit give\n\\[\n\\limsup_{\\mathcal D\\ni z\\to\\xi}H(z)\n\\le\n\\begin{cases}\nAG(\\xi)\\le0,&amp;|\\xi|=r,\\\\\nA(G(\\xi)-1)\\le0,&amp;\\xi\\in[u,v].\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\nThe second line includes both sides of the slit and its endpoints. At a zero of \\(f\\), the logarithm tends to \\(-\\infty\\).</p><p>The boundary-limsup maximum principle for subharmonic functions gives \\(H\\le0\\) on \\(\\mathcal D\\). Since \\(1\\in\\mathcal D\\),\n\\[\n\\log|f(1)|\\le-AG(1),\n\\]\nas required.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 8", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "There is an absolute positive integer \\(w\\) for which \\( \\|\\mu_w-U_N\\|_{\\mathrm{TV}}\\longrightarrow0 \\qquad(d\\to\\infty). \\) The convergence is uniform over deterministic initial decks.", "statement": "<p>Fourier summation for a fixed number of binary sweeps.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(N=2^d\\), and let \\(B_N\\) be the binary sweep law.</p><p class=\"li\">Put\n  \\[\n  K_\\lambda=\\mathbb E_{g\\sim B_N}\\rho_\\lambda(g),\n  \\qquad D_\\lambda=\\dim[\\lambda].\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Assume that for all sufficiently large \\(d\\), with a fixed absolute \\(g&gt;0\\),\n  \\[\n  \\|K_\\lambda\\|_{\\mathrm{op}}\\le D_\\lambda^{-g}\n  \\quad\\text{for every }\\lambda\\vdash N.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Assume the sign coefficient is zero.</p><p class=\"li\">For a positive integer \\(w\\), write \\(\\mu_w=B_N^{*w}\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There is an absolute positive integer \\(w\\) for which\n\\[\n\\|\\mu_w-U_N\\|_{\\mathrm{TV}}\\longrightarrow0\n\\qquad(d\\to\\infty).\n\\]\nThe convergence is uniform over deterministic initial decks.</p>", "proof": "<p>The convolution convention gives\n\\[\n\\widehat\\mu_w(\\lambda)=K_\\lambda^w.\n\\]\nCauchy–Schwarz and matrix Plancherel give the finite-group Fourier bound\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\|\\mu_w-U_N\\|_{\\mathrm{TV}}^2\n&amp;\\le\n\\frac14\\sum_{\\lambda\\ne(N)}\nD_\\lambda\\|K_\\lambda^w\\|_2^2\\\\\n&amp;\\le\n\\frac14\n\\sum_{\\lambda\\notin\\{(N),(1^N)\\}}\nD_\\lambda^{2-2gw}.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nThe second inequality uses sign cancellation and\n\\[\n\\|K_\\lambda^w\\|_2^2\n\\le D_\\lambda\\|K_\\lambda\\|_{\\mathrm{op}}^{2w}.\n\\]</p><p>Put\n\\[\n\\beta=2gw-2,\n\\qquad\nk(\\lambda)=\\min(N-\\lambda_1,N-\\lambda'_1).\n\\]\nFor fixed \\(1\\le k\\le N/3\\), a diagram with \\(k(\\lambda)=k\\) has first row \\(N-k\\) after transposition if necessary, and its remaining boxes form a partition of \\(k\\). There are at most \\(2\\cdot2^k\\) such diagrams. Proposition 1 gives\n\\[\nD_\\lambda\\ge e^{-1}(N/k)^k.\n\\]\nFor \\(k&gt;N/3\\), Proposition 1 gives\n\\[\n\\log D_\\lambda\\ge c_{\\mathrm{dim}}N\n\\]\nfor large \\(N\\), and there are at most \\(2^N\\) partitions of \\(N\\). Hence, for \\(\\beta&gt;0\\),\n\\[\n\\sum_{\\substack{\\lambda\\vdash N\\\\1\\le k(\\lambda)\\le N/3}}\nD_\\lambda^{-\\beta}\n\\le\n2e^\\beta\n\\sum_{k=1}^{\\lfloor N/3\\rfloor}\n[2(k/N)^\\beta]^k,\n\\]\nand\n\\[\n\\sum_{\\substack{\\lambda\\vdash N\\\\k(\\lambda)&gt;N/3}}\nD_\\lambda^{-\\beta}\n\\le\n\\exp((\\log2-\\beta c_{\\mathrm{dim}})N).\n\\]</p><p>For every fixed \\(k\\), the summand in the first bound tends to zero. Throughout its range it is at most\n\\[\n(2\\cdot3^{-\\beta})^k.\n\\]\nThus the first sum tends to zero when \\(\\beta&gt;\\log2/\\log3\\), by domination by a summable geometric sequence. The second tends to zero when \\(\\beta c_{\\mathrm{dim}}&gt;\\log2\\).</p><p>Choose an absolute integer \\(w\\) so that\n\\[\n2gw-2&gt;\n\\max\\left\\{\\frac{\\log2}{\\log3},\n\\frac{\\log2}{c_{\\mathrm{dim}}}\\right\\}.\n\\]\nThe total variation distance tends to zero. Translating the law by a deterministic initial deck leaves its distance from uniform unchanged, giving the stated uniformity.</p>"}], "edges": [[0, 1, "cite"], [2, 18, "cite"], [2, 24, "cite"], [3, 5, "cite"], [3, 8, "cite"], [4, 17, "cite"], [4, 18, "cite"], [5, 4, "cite"], [5, 7, "cite"], [5, 8, "cite"], [5, 9, "cite"], [6, 4, "cite"], [6, 7, "cite"], [6, 9, "cite"], [7, 4, "cite"], [8, 4, "cite"], [8, 9, "cite"], [9, 4, "cite"], [10, 4, "cite"], [11, 15, "cite"], [11, 16, "cite"], [12, 11, "cite"], [12, 13, "cite"], [13, 11, "cite"], [14, 17, "cite"], [14, 19, "cite"], [15, 16, "cite"], [16, 14, "part"], [17, 0, "cite"], [17, 21, "cite"], [18, 17, "cite"], [19, 17, "cite"], [19, 20, "cite"], [20, 17, "cite"], [21, 0, "cite"], [22, 21, "cite"], [23, 21, "cite"], [24, 0, "cite"]]});
